  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Mainly dry conditions expected through the weekend\nwith seasonable temperatures.\n\nSurface high pressure builds further over the region today with flow \naloft becoming more zonal. High pressure overhead will result in \nlight and generally variable wind patterns today allowing for local \nsea breezes in the coastal areas. Temperatures will be in seasonable \nranges with highs in the low to mid 80s. Another clear night will \nsupport radiational cooler allowing temperatures to fall into the \n50s to around 60 overnight. \n\nThe center of the surface high pushes offshore on Saturday shifting \nwinds to the east. With weak high pressure still in place, expect \nanother dry day with mainly sunny skies. Highs across the interior \nlikely range in the low to mid 80s while the onshore flow will keep \ntemperatures slightly cool (mid-upper 70s) along the immediate \neastern coastal areas. By Sunday, mid-level troughing arrives from \nwest. Most guidance also indicates a low pressure system passing \noffshore. As the NAM/RRFS are starting to come into range, they have \nbeen leaning more aggressive with bringing in a push of moisture \nfrom offshore later Sunday, particularly for the eastern portion of \nsouthern New England(Cape/Islands). Model soundings indicate a \nshallow layer of moisture in the lower levels supported by the \neasterly surface flow with drier mid-level air. This could \npotentially point to a layer of low clouds and even some patchy \ndrizzle in areas of eastern MA developing through the day on Sunday. \nOther model guidance is less aggressive, but still shows a \nsignal for moisture, although more faint. Due to the spread, \nthis is lower confidence at this time. Details will become more \nclear as we sit more solidly in the high-res guidance window. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Periodic chances for showers and thunderstorms\nlate Monday through mid-week, potentially lingering into\nThursday.\n\nThere is a good consensus across model guidance on a mid-level \ntrough positioning across the region for a good through at least \nlate-week next week. A series of shortwaves embedded in the flow are \nexpected to progress across the region through the week supporting \nperiodic chances for showers and thunderstorms. Flow becomes more \nsoutherly Monday with moisture gradually advecting into the region \nahead of the first shortwave. By Tuesday, precipitable water \nvalues rise to 1.50 to 2\". This will set up the region for \nperiodic chances for showers and thunderstorms, especially in \nthe late Monday through mid-week timeframe. There is potential \nfor the unsettled conditions to linger through Thursday if the \ntrough tracks slower. There is a fair amount of agreement among \nguidance that this won't be a complete wash-out with support for\nmore diurnally driven development of showers and thunderstorms \nin the afternoon/evenings. Given above normal precipitable water\namounts, localized heavy rainfall will be a possibility within \nany thunderstorms. There is a signal for potentially strong to\nsevere thunderstorms highlighted in the NSSL ML probabilities\nTue/Wed, given the higher instability and sufficient shear.\nSometimes to keep an eye on for now. Temperatures will likely \nhover between normal to just below normal for the first half of \nthe week.",
